<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Councils of Braga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">561, 572</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> were church councils held at Bracara Augusta, capital of the Suebic kingdom in Gallaecia. The first, in 561, condemned the Priscillianist heresy and settled church discipline after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>'s return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>; the second, in 572 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-braga">Martin of Braga</a>, organised the bishoprics of the kingdom. Further councils met there in the Visigothic period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/councils-of-braga.jpg" alt="Councils of Braga" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Braga-mapa_mediaval.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> was the metropolitan see of an ecclesiastical province and the chief bishopric of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in the Suevic period, and again in the High Middle Ages, when it lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>. A council said to have met there in 411 is probably an invention of the historian Frei Bernardo de Brito. The first council was presided over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a> and the second by Saint Martin of Dume, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-braga">Martin of Braga</a> is also known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> took part in the first council, which issued twenty-two decrees. All were to follow the same rite and readings, and to greet the people with Dominus vobiscum without the alterations of the Priscillianists. Mass was to follow the order sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Profuturus, and nothing was to be sung in church but the Psalms and texts of the Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. Priests who abstained from meat had to eat vegetables cooked with it, to avoid any suspicion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscillianism">Priscillianism</a>, and no one was to be buried inside a church.</p><p>The second council, held to increase the number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in Gallaecia, was attended by twelve bishops, from the suffragan sees of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> and from the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>, and issued ten decrees. Bishops were to inspect how priests administered the sacraments, catechumens were to receive twenty days of instruction before baptism, and no fee was to be taken for ordination, the chrism or the consecration of a church. The metropolitan was to announce the date of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, and anyone saying Mass without fasting was to lose his office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A third council met in 675 under Leodegisius in the reign of King Wamba and issued eight decrees, among them a ban on offering milk and grapes in place of bread and wine. Councils were held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> again in 1137, 1148, 1262, 1286, 1292, 1426 and 1470, and in 1566, when the council was meant to apply the reforms of the Council of Trent. Forty synods are also recorded there from the 13th century onwards.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Councils of Braga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Councils of Braga</b> of 561 and 572 organised the church.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-suebi">Kingdom of the Suebi</a></li></ul></section>
